review:
I thought of writing a really extensive review with all the issues I had with this game, but on second thought it didn't even deserve it, so I'll list some of the worst points that can maybe help people in doubt if Cyberpunk is worth their money.

1) The game is too easy. Even on very hard difficulty, I never found a challenging enemy or boss;
2) Mission design is really simplistic, like: talk to person A, go from place A to place B, wait for person A, talk to person A and person B over and over again;
3) Aside from Watson and Heywood, the map felt very empty and lifeless;
4) Many mission checks were bugged even years after the disastrous launch;
5) Paid DLC achievements;
6) Non-skippable dialogues and cutscenes, which is awful for replayability;
7) Janky and limited gameplay (better than other cdpr titles, though);
8) You need an external launcher in order to play the game.

Overall, if you really want it, the themes are good and the performance has been greatly improved from launch, but I wouldn't buy it without a heavy discount  and highly recommend playing with mods to circumvent some of the issues listed above.

I love this game; pretty much everything about it. Story, music, setting, I can't stop playing! So why am I leaving a negative review?

The amount of bugs (and their severity) is insane, even now when the game should have already matured. Most of them aren't game breaking, but even so, they are very noticeable.
- NPC animations bugging, sometimes when they die they just stay in their last alive position;
- Vehicles teleporting when you get in;
- Bosses t-posing instead of attacking you (!)
- Saving and loading tends to bug scripted sequences;
- Scripted cars having their wheels bugged and going slower than they should;
- Stationary radios do not have all radio stations (e.g. Samizdat Radio);
- plus many more that I forgot about...

As a game dev myself, I struggle to wrap my head around how a game can possibly be coded this badly. Literal Roblox games are more polished than this. If the game logic is broken beyond saving and a rewrite is too expensive, introduce failsafes. E.g. if an NPC has died, double check after a second if they have actually fallen. Doesn't seem that hard to me.

On top of that, some minor inconveniences that aren't bugs:
- The physics of scripted vehicles (specifically the ones that you ride) are so awful they make me want to gouge my eyes out;
- The journal ignores the fact you deselected a quest after doing a gig;
- Some mechanics feel like cheating even considering the high-tech world setting.

I love this game so much that I don't even want to play it.

Literally, I can't bring myself to finish it. I'm too scared to know how on earth my story will end, and I don't want to face the heartbreak that comes with it. ;O;

Apart from that though, it's incredible. Superb. F*cking AMAZING, and has inspired so much creativity within me. PLAY IT IF YOU HAVEN'T ALREADY, DON'T HOLD BACK

